                        "glosses": [
                            "A native of Switzerland; a Swiss."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4cc33f15-5dd8-4fe5-baee-9486c10e6c07",
                                "sense_id": "f3e5925f-331c-3225-9a3a-ac8b47a845a8",
                                "text": "Being impossible to conclude and accord any agreement betweene the Pope and the King of France, the Swissers came to serue the Pope, of whose valour and power the French stood much in feare."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c5ebe02c-7c6c-4d95-9743-3744cec9def6",
                                "sense_id": "f3e5925f-331c-3225-9a3a-ac8b47a845a8",
                                "text": "A Spaniard can not well brooke to feede after our fashion, nor we endure to drinke as the Swizzers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "681fe17c-cbf8-4b2c-b507-8f3bd3c8852e",
                                "sense_id": "f3e5925f-331c-3225-9a3a-ac8b47a845a8",
                                "text": "Where is my Swissers, let them guard the doore."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "98d71b2c-8ba2-4aa2-abf1-5b601fb56973",
                                "sense_id": "f3e5925f-331c-3225-9a3a-ac8b47a845a8",
                                "text": "The Austrian Princes and the Swissers, I have still heard, are from father to sonne, hereditary and irreconcilable enimies."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
